    "### 4.2 Semantic Analysis: Syntagmatic Collocations vs. Paradigmatic Synonyms\n",
    "\n",
    "The nearest neighbor comparison highlights a foundational distinction in vector semantics:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **GloVe captures Paradigmatic & Taxonomic Equivalence:**\n",
    "   - Because GloVe was trained on 6 billion tokens spanning Wikipedia and Gigaword news, words that share interchangeable syntactic and semantic slots cluster together.\n",
    "   - `football` $\\to$ `soccer` (0.87), `basketball` (0.86), `league` (0.82), `rugby` (0.80), `hockey` (0.78) (co-hyponyms in the sports taxonomy).\n",
    "   - `government` $\\to$ `administration` (0.79), `governments` (0.77), `officials` (0.76), `authorities` (0.74).\n",
    "   - `software` $\\to$ `computer` (0.84), `hardware` (0.79), `microsoft` (0.78), `applications` (0.74).\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **Skip-gram captures Episodic Syntagmatic Collocations & Corpus Discourse:**\n",
    "   - Trained directly on the BBC corpus, Skip-gram models the narrative discourse of 2004–2005 British news.\n",
    "   - `minister` $\\to$ `prime` (0.86), `silvio` (0.69 - Berlusconi), `yulia` (0.68), `tymoshenko` (0.66), `ahern` (0.64 - Bertie Ahern). These reflect specific political actors frequently mentioned with \"prime minister\".\n",
    "   - `champion` $\\to$ `hardenne` (0.72), `henin` (0.72), `maurice` (0.71), `justine` (0.71) — directly capturing Belgian tennis star Justine Henin-Hardenne.\n",
    "   - `music` $\\to$ `urban` (0.61), `xtra` (0.59 - BBC Radio 1Xtra), `napster` (0.56), `downloading` (0.56) — reflecting the digital music piracy and streaming shift in the mid-2000s."
