    "## 7. Synthesis, Error Analysis, and Limitations\n",
    "\n",
    "### 7.1 Model Comparison & Downstream Performance Verdict\n",
    "- **Empirical Accuracy:** Both Word2Vec Skip-gram ($97.53\\% - 97.75\\%$) and GloVe ($97.30\\%$) achieve remarkable classification performance on the 5-domain news dataset.\n",
    "- **Why In-Domain Skip-gram Slightly Outperformed GloVe:** \n",
    "  Despite being trained on a vastly smaller corpus (2,225 documents vs. 6 billion tokens), Skip-gram was trained directly on the target distribution. It encodes specific collocations, UK governmental terminology, and named entities (e.g., `blair`, `brown`, `chancellor`, `tory`) that directly delineate the news domains.\n",
    "- **Where GloVe Excels:** \n",
    "  GloVe displays significantly higher semantic coherence and cleanly organized taxonomic relationships (e.g., co-hyponyms in sports and technology), making it far more robust to general out-of-domain linguistic variations.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 7.2 Error Analysis & Domain Boundary Overlap\n",
    "The confusion matrices indicate that errors predominantly cluster between **Politics and Business**:\n",
    "- News articles covering government budgets, treasury taxation policies, interest rate adjustments, and state pension regulations frequently invoke both political and economic terminology simultaneously.\n",
    "- When centroid pooling averages all word vectors in a document, the resulting vector lies in the overlapping boundary between the Politics and Business manifolds.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 7.3 Fundamental Limitations of Static Word Embeddings\n",
    "1. **The Polysemy Dilemma (Static Vector Assignment):**\n",
    "   - Both Skip-gram and GloVe conflate all senses of a polysemous word into a single vector. For example, `apple` (fruit vs. technology giant), `bank` (financial institution vs. river bank), or `share` (stock security vs. verb of division) receive a single static embedding that represents an unnatural centroid of competing semantic senses.\n",
    "2. **Information Loss from Mean Centroid Pooling:**\n",
    "   - Averaging word vectors treats documents as a continuous \"bag-of-embeddings\", discarding word order, syntactic structure, negation (e.g., \"not good\" averages identically to \"good\"), and discourse hierarchy.\n",
    "   - Long documents suffer from dilution, where dominant background vocabulary dampens the contribution of discriminative keywords.\n",
    "3. **Out-of-Vocabulary (OOV) Vulnerability:**\n",
    "   - Word2Vec and GloVe cannot construct vectors for unseen words, rare acronyms, or novel morphological variants at test time. Subword-based tokenizers (BPE, WordPiece) or character n-gram models (FastText) are required to alleviate OOV failures.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 7.4 Modern Architectural Directions\n",
    "Modern NLP circumvents these limitations through **contextualized representations** (e.g., BERT, RoBERTa, DeBERTa, and autoregressive LLMs). These architectures compute dynamic token representations parameterized by self-attention mechanisms, generating unique contextual vectors conditioned on the entire sentence context."
   ]
